welcome to this presentation about  reinforcement learning  um we are going to talk about uh  offline enforcement learning in  particular what's possible to achieve  with the linear predictors from  exponential lower bounds to polynomial  upper bounds  first of all what is offline  reinforcement learning  well  [Music]  you might be more familiar with online  reinforcement learning in online  reinforcement learning  which is the standard reinforcement  learning setting  there is an interaction between an agent  and an environment the agent submits  submits action to the environment and he  receives next states and rewards  in the offline reinforcement learning  setting we assume that this interaction  has already occurred and we are given a  data set  of  state action rewards and next states  and then the data set we are going to  give it to the batch algorithm and then  we are going to ask questions to the  batch algorithm a typical question could  be for example to extract from the data  set and you have optimal policy there  could be other  tasks for example you can do  policy evaluation you can ask to  evaluate the value of another policy but  for this talk we are going to focus on  extracting and your optimal policy  now  we are going to consider the function  approximation setting  in particular we are going to assume  that  the  action value function  can be represented linearly  as an inner product between a feature  extractor that you do know and some  parameter that is unknown to you and  you're trying to  figure out the parameter  there are  two parts  of this talker one is a  fundamental information theoretic lower  bounds  which might be surprising if you're  familiar with the  you know statistical learning component  and then we are going to see what's  possible to do if you have some extra  assumption that is  i would say  another reinforcement of my living  assumption particular bellman closure  and we are trying to see we will try to  see what's possible to achieve under  those additional assumptions  so lower bounds  it turns out that  realizability is insufficient  in reinforcement learning  in particular  let's place ourselves in  in the best possible case so let's  assume  we have no approximation error the  actual mdp  has an exact linear representation for  the optimal action value function  uh we are in the well specified case we  are given  a data set that is very explorative in  particular it does cover in future space  pretty much every direction  and we are going to give you as much  data as you want  even in the limit of infinite data  now  if you're doing uh if you're trying to  just fit a linear predictor  uh in machine learning you know the  answer here is trivial there is no  problem i can  um  i can recover the value of the test data  in reinforcement learning unfortunately  well it depends what you're modeling and  if you're modeling  the action value function unfortunately  the problem turns out to be extremely  complicated  and  you can show a fundamental information  to vertical lower buttons  that says that even if you have this  very strong assumption um perfect  realizability normal specification  infinite data  very good conditioning still you can  find a class of mdps where you can learn  very very little why is this even  happening  well it's happening because  somehow you can  put some sort of neural network into  your mdp  and the dynamics can be very complicated  and the actual value function ends up  being linear but in reality it is  concealing a very complicated object  which is in fact a neural network so  this is not enough for learning we have  to add some extra assumption  now if you take a look at the rld  showdown you're going to see  a bunch of different assumptions we're  going to make one that is very  reasonable batman closure i am now going  to  go into the details of these but this is  slightly stronger than  realizability  under visa you can start asking  interesting questions for example what  happens  if i don't have a very good covariance  matrix for example what happens if i  don't have a lot of data in a certain  direction which might be uh which might  happen to be the direction of the  optimal policy what happens if i of  course have you know a finite amount of  data  and maybe those data  they're being generated by some  adversarial procedure right maybe even  maliciously  well under those conditions we are able  to create an algorithm that can find  a very good policy with high probability  and he scales with the standard  writer some quantity divided by square  root of  n is the amount of samples and c  represents  the  um i would say the cont the conditioning  that you have about your problem and  this is just something specific to the  data set  let's delve a bit more into the  algorithm what's the goal what's the  setting and what's the intuition for it  um  well the setting is that we're going to  give some data set to the bas algorithm  and we ask  the algorithm to return  a policy as good as possible but with  very high probability so we want to be  sure  that the policy that is returned by the  algorithm performs as advertised by the  algorithm let me make a concrete example  to understand the formulation  concrete example is this  we have two policy  two policies a and b  and we want to find the best one to  return we have some available data we  are going to  compute for example the empirical  average of the return  and we want to return the one that is  highest  but just knowing some empirical leverage  is not going to be enough because this  could be widely uh inaccurate so  second thing that we could do is try to  construct some confidence interval with  high probability with the probability  that you specify as input to your  algorithm  and then you can start reasoning with  those  and you could say hey i really want to  be confident about what i return  and so i return a  policy a because sure it has a lower  empirical mean but i am way more  confident in particular in a world case  scenario the performance of policy is  going to be better than the performance  of policy b  now if you spend 2-3 minutes thinking  about this problem which is very simple  i have just two actions  you will realize that i am solving  really a maximum problem i am trying to  find the  the better alternative between a and b  and i am trying to see what's the worst  case uh  outcome  for the alternative uh that they choose  now you can think about this in the  sequential settings in the reinforcement  learning setting  and that's what we do for for the world  we extend that intuition and  we design an algorithm that is able to  pessimistically predict the value of  different policies  and that's what we call a critic  and then we're going to couple it with  an actor and the actor is going to  maximize over the policies  and  this is you know it has a nice framework  because  the critic is really trying to be  adversarial compared to the actor and it  is using the data set  and it is trying to  um  provide pessimistic evaluation to the  policy that is selected by the actor and  so this is a classical you know  adversarial  objective function and what's the right  tool to use for the maximization well  any algorithm essentially from  online learning here we use a  mirror the center  and  the whole procedure is what we call  an actor critic method in reinforcement  learning  and so in terms of main results  we  discovered that really after critic  are a very natural way to solve the max  mean problem in reinforcement learning  and somehow they are the natural  approach  to um  for learning from offline data in a very  robust way  the procedure that we have happens to be  mini max optimum it is computationally  tractable  and um it has a bunch of other  interesting things  uh more you know future directions  well here we examine the linear setting  it would be nice to uh take a look at  this and see what happens with more  general function approximations  another technical question could be  can we do the exploration counterpart  can we try to leverage some of this idea  in the exploration setting where i have  to collect the data myself  and can i try to relax some of these  assumptions that we make in  offline reinforcement learning  and with this i conclude and i thank you  for your attention  okay time for one quick question while  angel sets up  yeah  so  i think i missed one part so this uh  maximum formulation you use it as your  benchmark or it's just a strategy to  actually reach a more  canonical benchmark for a rainforest on  learning it is uh basically the starting  point of the algorithm  so we use it for constructing  we solve this problem okay but the  guarantee is not in terms of this  objective is it  the guarantee  is going to be in terms of this  objective yes you have to you know  implement these things somehow right and  so you have to speak about confidence  intervals but  yes  it will be in terms of visual stuff  next up is angela

